              "status": "full",
              "source": "Chapter lengkap",
              "content": [
                "Elara spent the next day among villagers who could not agree on which day it was. A mother remembered burying her son, then turned to see him carrying firewood through the square. An old man kept asking for breakfast he had already eaten nine times. Two children had learned to use the loop to steal honey cakes, then forgotten the theft before guilt could mature.",
                "The Saintess knelt in the chapel and raised Aegis of the Weeping Goddess over a dozen frightened families. The sanctuary formed late. For three breaths, nothing happened. Then gold-white light unfolded from the floor like a flower that had been waiting for permission. It protected them, but its edge flickered behind real time, always arriving after danger should have passed.",
                "Elara trembled, not from exhaustion, but from helplessness. 'My prayer is late,' she told Helena when the villagers slept. 'I have healed wounds, curses, poison, grief when grief touched the body. But time does not ask to be healed. It refuses to stand still long enough for mercy.'",
                "Helena looked toward the chapel window, where upward snow scratched faintly at the glass. 'Then do not heal time,' she said. 'Hold the people while I ask why time is wounded.' Elara studied her for a moment, then nodded. It was the first agreement of the arc: Helena would face the law. Elara would keep the living from breaking beneath its answer."
              ],
